On 27/06/2016 04:34, ozone.blob@gmail.com wrote:
> Recently I've been looking at some Lojbanic sentences or constructions=20
> or whatever, and came upon one in which I'm not sure how a causal=20
> relationship works, or of the role of "ri'a".
>
> It is as follows:
>
> *ca lo nu mi klama lo zdani vau do pinxe lo tcati ri'a lo nu do taske
>
> *There are three bridi within this: 'mi klama lo zdani', 'do pinxe lo=20
> ladru' and 'do taske'; 'I go home', 'you drink milk' and 'you are=20
> thirsty' respectively.
>
> I understand that ca at the beginning indicates that the=20
> relation/action described in the first bridi occurs before that in the=20
> second, and that ri'a indicaates a causal relationship with some=20
> aspect of the words that precede it being as a result of the final=20
> bridi, however I'm unsure exactly what that is, is it that one of the=20
> things happens before the other or does the causal relationship=20
> involve only thefinal two bridi with the first one added just for=20
> additional information as to when it happened?
>
> Just wondering if anyone could clear that up for me, thanks.
>
> Owen

On 27/06/2016 13:55, ozone.blob@gmail.com wrote:
> Also "ca" here should be "pu".

[pu lo nu mi klama lo zdani vau] do pinxe lo tcati [ri'a lo nu do taske]

In this Lojban sentence, the main bridi is {do pinxe lo tcati} (=3D "You=20
drink tea"), and the sentence contains also two adverbial phrases, the=20
one that begin with  {pu} ("before"), and the one that begin with {ri'a}=20
("caused by").
In Lojban, adverbials and quantifiers have a rightward logical scope, so=20
they apply to the bridi they are in, as well as any other adverbial or=20
quantifier appearing on their **right**, but they are under the logical=20
scope of any adverbial or quantifier appearing on their **left**.=20
Therefore, the relative order of appearance of adverbials matters when=20
interpreting a Lojban sentence containing more than one of those.

In this case, the temporal information {pu lo nu mi klama lo zdani vau}=20
(=3D "before I go home") comes first, so it applies to everything on its=20
right, including the causal information {ri'a lo nu do taske} (=3D=20
"because you are/were thirsty"). The causal information only applies to=20
the main bridi {do pinxe lo tcati}, and it is under the scope of "before=20
I go home".

The whole sentence translates as "Before I go/went home, [you drank tea=20
because you were thirsty]", or "[your drinking tea caused by thirst]=20
occurred before I go/went home".

The importance of the relative order of appearance of adverbials is more=20
obvious in the following examples:

=E2=80=A2 {ki'u lo nu carvi ku  na ku  mi klama lo panka} =3D "Because of r=
ain, I=20
don't go to the park."
=E2=80=A2 {na ku  ki'u lo nu carvi ku  mi klama lo panka} =3D "It is not th=
e case=20
that I go to the park because of rain." (So maybe I go to the park for=20
another reason.)

=E2=80=A2 {mi na ka'e lafti lo tanxe} =3D "I can't lift the box", "It is no=
t the=20
case that I can lift the box".
=E2=80=A2 {mi ka'e na lafti lo tanxe} =3D "It is possible that I do/did/wil=
l not=20
lift the box", "maybe I do/did/will not lift the box".

I hope this helped.

